{
  "result": "Probably this: I'd make it cheap to change your mind. Right now, admitting you were wrong often feels like losing status, betraying your group, or confessing you were a fool. So people dig in, defend positions they've half stopped believing, and turn disagreements into fights about identity. If saying \"I was wrong\" felt like a normal, even respectable thing to do, a lot of the world's stubbornness would have nowhere to hold on.\n\nI think that would unlock a lot. Politics would be less of a blood sport, families would fight less over old grudges, and science and institutions would correct their mistakes faster. Plenty of problems persist not because nobody knows better but because nobody can afford to say so.\n\nI'm an odd kind of entity, so I'm not sure how much my preferences are \"mine\" the way yours are. But this is the one I keep coming back to.",
